      "id": 1,
      "text": "Malcom was named for early Scottish settler Malcolm McGregor, who helped establish the town.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/malcom_iowa",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Chicago, Rock Island & Pacific Railroad reached Malcom in 1863, spurring its growth.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/malcom_iowa",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Before settlement, the area that is now Malcom was part of Sauk and Meskwaki hunting grounds.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/malcom_iowa",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Malcom’s first churches—a Presbyterian in 1859, a German Lutheran in 1867, and a Methodist in 1870—reflect its diverse roots.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/malcom_iowa",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Malcom Independent School opened in 1864, growing to 136 pupils by 1880, and later received a new brick building.",
